Carafe: with X87SinglePrecision and X87ReducedPrecision, x87 values are kept and computed as 32-bit floats instead
of doubles. Direct3D 9 sets the x87 precision control to 24 bits unless a game asks it not to, so a Direct3D 9
game's float math already rounds every result to a float. In games' x87 code most operands are floats in memory,
and with doubles every load, every arithmetic operand and every store took an fcvt on the dependency chain: about
a fifth of all emitted instructions. Registers keep their doubles in the thread state, so FSAVE, FXSAVE, MMX and the
slow path see the same layout, and values are converted where a block loads or stores them there. FSIN, FCOS,
FPTAN, FPATAN, FPREM, FSCALE, FYL2X, F2XM1 and FXTRACT run in double and round the result to a float. FIST and FISTP
convert from double as before. Off by default; wrong for games that rely on double precision.
MIT, as the files it changes.

